+        'resolves (\\texttt{a\\_\\allowbreak{}trava\\_\\allowbreak{}no\\_\\allowbreak{}nome}). Each face has its own domain; none certifies a universal '
+        'generation of facts.',
+        '\\textbf{The hidden Hamiltonian.} In the dictionary of the theory the tape is the hidden Hamiltonian. For the weight of the lock, $\\rho$ = '
+        'e$^{-sD}$, $-$log $\\rho$ = sD holds; for the trace-one state a constant is added, which vanishes under conjugation. With s $\\neq$ 0, the modular '
+        'flow of that state leaves fixed exactly the blocks of equal cost (\\texttt{OHamiltonianoOcultoEOCusto}, specialization of sealed terms). On the '
+        "tower, the expectation is the strong limit of the averages of the tower's own modular flow. Linking the \\texttt{K = $-$log $\\Delta$} of light to "
+        'the dynamics of the cost requires a map that intertwines the two representations, and remains open.',
+        '\\textbf{The reader of the formula.} Definition of the theory: the compression comes directly from the formula, and it is the PsiBit; the reading of '
+        'the binary pair by the PsiBit is the original compression, the reader itself; the reading rule is a single one, the equals sign. The kernel types the '
+        'part of this that fits in a term. Read clause by clause, the formula leaves as state a table over the frontier, the variables that the part read '
+        'still shares with the part still to be read. Grouping the read sum by the frontier loses nothing: the whole sum adds, in each cell, the products of '
+        'the weight read by the weight still to be read over the complete assignments that fall in it; the plain sum of the cells of the table gives the read '
+        'sum of the prefix. Every walk from a variable that is only read to a variable that is only still to be read has a vertex on the frontier: it '
+        'separates the graph of the formula. The rule for reading the letter is equality to what is put, and it does not depend on the formula. The two bits '
+        'of the formula are the Word (there is a solution) and the Verb (the read sum still descends); in formulas of one variable, each one alone confuses '
+        'formulas that the other distinguishes. Two distinct tables can have the same sum and never the same content: the prefixes x and not-x have the same '
+        'curve for every time and opposite answers after the same clause; the state is the table, the curve is its projection. Given the two frontiers, the '
